[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Tutorial: OpenID Connect (OIDC) and Cloud Provider Authentication in GitHub Actions"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Traditional methods of authenticating GitHub Actions workflows to cloud providers rely on long-lived secrets or cloud credentials stored in GitHub Secrets. This approach introduces security risks, such as secret leakage and the operational overhead of key rotation. OpenID Connect (OIDC) solves this by allowing GitHub Actions to request a short-lived access token directly from your cloud provider (AWS, Azure, or GCP) at runtime."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Prerequisites and Architecture"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before configuring your cloud provider, understand the trust relationship flow:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"GitHub's OIDC provider acts as an Identity Provider (IdP) and signs a JSON Web Token (JWT) containing metadata about the workflow (repository, branch, environment)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Your cloud provider trusts GitHub's IdP based on a configured thumbprint and issuer URL."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The workflow exchanges the GitHub JWT for a short-lived cloud IAM role session."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"AWS Configuration"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To configure AWS, create an Identity Provider in IAM using the provider URL 'https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com' and the audience 'sts.amazonaws.com'. Then, create an IAM Role with a trust policy that restricts assumption to your specific GitHub repository."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "Federated": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_ID:oidc-provider/token.actions.githubusercontent.com"
      },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:aud": "sts.amazonaws.com"
        },
        "StringLike": {
          "token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub": "repo:YOUR_ORG/YOUR_REPO:*"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Azure Configuration"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Azure, you use Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) App Registrations and User-Assigned Managed Identities. Create a Managed Identity, navigate to 'Federated credentials', and add a new credential. Set the issuer to 'https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com', choose the subject identifier based on your repository structure, and target 'api://AzureADTokenExchange'."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"GCP Configuration"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"For Google Cloud Platform, set up Workload Identity Federation. Create a Workload Identity Pool and a Provider of type OpenID Connect (OIDC). Configure the issuer URI as 'https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com' and map the attribute conditions to assert the GitHub repository."},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"GitHub Actions Workflow Implementation"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once your cloud provider is configured, you must request the 'id-token' permission in your workflow file. Below is an example demonstrating authentication against AWS."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"name: OIDC Cloud Authentication
on:
  push:
    branches: [ "main" ]

permissions:
  id-token: write
  contents: read

jobs:
  assume-role:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Git Checkout
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Authenticate to AWS via OIDC
        uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4
        with:
          role-to-assume: arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_ID:role/YourGitHubActionsRole
          aws-region: us-east-1

      - name: Verify AWS Access
        run: |
          aws sts get-caller-identity"},{"order":18,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always restrict trust policies to specific branches, tags, or environments rather than trusting an entire organization or repository blindly."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage GitHub Environments to enforce manual approvals and environment-specific deployment restrictions before OIDC tokens are issued."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Audit cloud IAM logs regularly to monitor successful and failed AssumeRole requests originating from GitHub Actions."}]
